HOME_HERO = f"""<section class="hero"><div><p class="kicker">Post oak · Salt &amp; pepper · Flat pack steel</p><h1>Texas barbecue, cut down to fit the tailgate</h1><p>You don't need a trailer-mounted offset to cook like a Texan. A small flat pack charcoal grill, a sack of lump, a few splits of post oak and some patience get you fajitas at the stadium, links at deer camp and real beef on a gravel bar. These guides show how, honestly: what a small lidless grill does well, and how to adapt brisket, sausage and ribs when it can't do a 14-hour cook.</p><p><a class="btn" href="{FULL}">See the Full Size Slot Grill</a> <a class="btn ghost" href="/building-a-texas-coal-bed.html">Start with the fire</a></p></div><img src="{IMG['full10']}" alt="Slot Grill Full Size cooking over charcoal"></section>
<section class="hsec"><h2>Built in Buna, Texas</h2><p>Every guide here is cooked on the <a href="{COMPACT}">Slot Grill</a>, a tab-and-slot carbon steel charcoal grill that Keith makes in Buna, in Southeast Texas. It comes in a Compact and a Full Size, with optional griddle plates, and packs flat behind a truck seat.</p></section>"""
